<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmedabad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmedabad is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the Sabarmati river. It was founded in 1411 by Ahmad Shah I of the Gujarat Sultanate as his capital and became famous for its mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stepwell">stepwells</a> and textile trade. Later a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mills, it was where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> founded his Sabarmati Ashram, and its old city became India's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage city in 2017.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmedabad.jpg" alt="Ahmedabad" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tarun802 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sabarmati_riverside.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ahmedabad lies in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, on the banks of the Sabarmati River, and covers 505 km2. It is 25 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandhinagar">Gandhinagar</a>, the state capital, known as its twin city. The city is almost flat, and the Sabarmati frequently dried up in summer until water from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> was diverted into it. Three lakes lie within the city limits: Kankaria, Vastrapur and Chandola. Kankaria was developed as an artificial lake by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Gujarat, Qutb-ud-din, in 1451.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>An early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil</a> settlement called Ashaval is thought to have stood here. According to Merutunga, Karna, the Chaulukya ruler of Anhilwada, defeated Ashaval and founded a city nearby called Karnavati, whose exact location is not known. Chaulukya rule lasted until the 13th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> came under the Vaghela dynasty of Dholka, and in the 14th century under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. In the early 15th century the governor Zafar Khan Muzaffar made himself independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-shah-i">Muzaffar Shah I</a>.</p><p>His grandson Sultan Ahmad Shah I moved the capital from Anhilvada in 1411 and laid the foundation of a walled city on the Sabarmati on 26 February 1411 at Manek Burj, named after the saint Maneknath. As was customary, the city was named after the sultan; other accounts say it was named after four Muslim saints called Ahmed. A British force under James Hartley captured it in 1780, during the First Anglo-Maratha War, and handed it back at the war's end. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> took it over in 1818, and a railway link to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> opened in 1864. The city became the home of a growing textile industry and was nicknamed the Manchester of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ahmedabad is the most populous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and the seat of the Gujarat High Court; the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 5,570,585 people. Under Ahmed Shah, builders fused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> craftsmanship with Persian architecture, and many mosques were built in this Indo-Saracenic style. A pol is the typical housing cluster of the old city, which was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in July 2017.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahmedabad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sultans built lavishly, above all in <b>Ahmedabad</b>, founded by Ahmad Shah I on the Sabarmati River and styled Shahr-i-Mu'azzam, &quot;the great city&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1918 Gandhi fasted to support low-paid workers striking at an Indian-owned mill in <b>Ahmedabad</b>, which won a settlement.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li><li><blockquote>The oldest known manuscript that contains it, at the B. J. Institute in <b>Ahmedabad</b>, is dated 1835 (Vikram Samvat 1892), though K. K. Shastri reported examining an early 18th-century one.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vaishnava-jana-to">Vaishnava Jana To</a></li><li><blockquote>Surat became the headquarters of the directorate of Suratte, which also oversaw posts at <b>Ahmedabad</b>, Agra and Cambay.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-suratte">Dutch Suratte</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Marathas took <b>Ahmedabad</b> in 1753 he secured Radhanpur and nearby districts as his own principality.</blockquote><a href="/state/radhanpur-state">Radhanpur State</a></li></ul></section>
